00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:06.500
In the last video, you saw how very deep neural networks can have the problems of vanishing

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.480
and exploding gradients.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.780
It turns out that a partial solution to this doesn't solve it entirely, but helps a lot,

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:19.300
is better or more careful choice of the random initialization for your neural network.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:23.300
To understand this, let's start with the example of initializing the weights for a

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:27.740
single neuron, and then we'll go on to generalize this to a deep network.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.740
Let's go through this with an example with just a single neuron, and then we'll talk

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.420
about a deep net later.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.460
So the single neuron, you might input four features, x1 through x4, and then you have

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.100
some a equals g of z, and then it outputs some y.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.140
And later on for a deep net, these inputs will be some layer a of l.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:52.100
But for now, let's just call this x for now.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:01:04.380
So z is going to be equal to w1 x1 plus w2 x2 plus dot dot dot plus wn xn.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:08.780
And let's set b equals 0, so let's just ignore b for now.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:16.220
So in order to make z not blow up and not become too small, you notice that the larger

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:22.380
n is, the smaller you want wi to be, right?

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:28.460
Because z is a sum of wi xi, and so if you're adding up a lot of these terms, you want

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:31.100
each of these terms to be smaller.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:38.780
One reasonable thing to do would be to set the variance of wi to be equal to 1 over

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:44.260
n, where n is the number of input features that's going into a neuron.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:50.500
So in practice, what you can do is set the weight matrix w for a certain layer to be

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:59.180
np dot random dot rand n, you know, and then whatever the shape of the matrix is, you

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.660
fold this out here.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:11.260
And then times square root of 1 over the number of features that are fed into each neuron

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:16.660
in layer L. So it's going to be n of L minus 1, because that's the number of units that

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:20.300
are feeding into each of the units in layer L.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:26.780
It turns out that if you're using a ReLU activation function, then rather than 1 over n, it turns

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.900
out that setting the variance to 2 over n works a little bit better.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.380
So you often see that in initialization, especially if you're using a ReLU activation function.

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:43.620
So if gL of z is the ReLU of z, oh, and depending on how familiar you are with random variables,

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.420
it turns out that something, a Gaussian random variable, and then multiplying it by square

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:53.660
root of this, that sets the variance to be equal to this thing, to be 2 over n.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:59.700
And the reason I went from n to this n superscript L minus 1 was in this example

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.580
with logistic regression, which is that n input features, but in the more general

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:11.180
case, layer L would have nL minus 1 inputs each of the units in that layer.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:18.100
So if the input features or the intuit activations are roughly mean 0 and standard variance, and

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:23.500
variance 1, then this would cause z to also be taken on a similar scale.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:29.660
And this doesn't solve, but it definitely helps reduce the vanishing and exploding

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.860
gradients problem, because it's trying to set each of the weight matrices w, you know,

00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:37.300
so that it's not too much bigger than 1 and not too much less than 1.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.420
So it doesn't explode or vanish too quickly.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.660
I've just mentioned some other variance.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:49.580
The version we just described is assuming a ReLU activation function, and it's by a paper

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:50.820
by Hoe et al.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:57.780
A few other variants, if you are using a 10H activation function, then there's a paper

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:02.780
that shows that instead of using the constant 2, it's better to use the constant 1.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:11.420
And so 1 over this instead of 2, so you multiply by the square root of this.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:16.900
So this square root term will replace this term.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:21.940
And you use this if you're using a 10H activation function.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:25.780
This is called Xavier initialization.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:30.140
And another version worked out by Josje Benjour and his colleagues, you might see in some

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:39.580
papers, but is to use this formula, which has some other theoretical justification.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:43.900
But I would say if you're using a ReLU activation function, which is really the most common

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:47.420
activation function, I would use this formula.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.220
If you're using 10H, you could try this version instead.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.060
And some authors will also use this.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:57.260
But in practice, I think all of these formulas just give you a starting point.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:01.660
It gives you a default value to use for the variance of the initialization of your weight

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:02.900
matrices.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:08.260
If you wish, the variance here, this variance parameter, could be another thing that you

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.380
could tune with your hyperparameters.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:16.020
So you could have another parameter that multiplies into this formula and tune that

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.780
multiplier as part of your hyperparameter search.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:25.700
Sometimes the tuning that hyperparameter has a modest size effect is not one of the

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.100
first hyperparameters I would usually try to tune.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.140
But I've also seen some problems with tuning this helps a reasonable amount.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:39.740
But this is usually lower down for me in terms of how important it is relative to the

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.580
other hyperparameters you could tune.

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:46.140
So I hope that gives you some intuition about the problem of vanishing or exploding

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:50.740
gradients, as well as how choosing a reasonable scaling for how you initialize

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:51.540
the weights.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:56.100
Hopefully that makes your weights, you'll not explode too quickly and not decay to

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.060
zero too quickly.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.980
So you can train a reasonably deep network without the weights or the gradients

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.500
exploding or vanishing too much.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.300
When you train deep networks, this is another trick that will help you make

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.260
your neural networks train much more quickly.
